views {
  view casework_architecture {
    title 'Casework / 01. Application architecture'
    description '''
      Registry Casework has two standalone deployment profiles. Hosted decisions remain
      entirely inside Casework. Source-backed work adds the BReg adapter while BReg keeps
      current visibility and every registry mutation. One project may enable either surface
      or both. A source-backed project may also enable bounded institutional task grants;
      Casework signs short-lived task assertions while the external identity provider issues access
      tokens and each resource retains authorization. Human users normally work through a
      separately deployed application host; a Requester service calls the hosted API directly.
      PostgreSQL owns coordination state and durable intent, while the external keyed journal
      retains published audit records.
    '''
    include casework_staff, casework_app, casework_client, casework_requester, taskAgent, issuer, casework, casework_postgres, casework_audit_journal, breg
    include
      breg -> casework with {
        title 'Sends event hints and checks task status'
        description 'Each status check causes Casework to reread BReg through its bounded service reader; source failure fails the check.'
      }
    global predicate casework_storage
    exclude breg -> issuer
  }

  view casework_runtime of casework {
    title 'Casework / 02. Runtime roles and responsibilities'
    description '''
      All nested components run in one process. Ordinary authentication selects one Casework
      profile; Staff, Supervisor and Administrator also require the configured human-identity claim.
      Current directory membership is enforced by the coordination, hosted and task-grant
      components for their operations. It never supplies source authority. Optional task-grant
      machine routes use separate actor-kind, client, audience and
      scope checks without selecting a human profile. Source-backed item assembly performs a
      caller-scoped source read under the explicitly selected source profile. The BReg adapter is
      the implemented instance of the source-neutral contract. Its private_key_jwt service reader
      has an exact resource and scopes independent of human callers. Routing values exist only
      while an observation is evaluated. Stored routing explains the matched authored rule without
      retaining predicate values. Clock calculations pin policy and holiday inputs; workers reread
      the source and fence occurrence, revision and calculation generations before recording an
      effect.
    '''
    include casework.*, casework_postgres, casework_audit_journal, breg, taskAgent
    include
      breg -> casework.api with {
        title 'Sends event hints and checks task status'
        description 'Each status check causes Casework to reread BReg through its bounded service reader; source failure fails the check.'
      }
  }

  dynamic view casework_task_approval {
    #optional #taskAuthority
    title 'Casework / 03. A human approves bounded institutional work'
    description '''
      This optional path applies only to source-backed items and stops after the immutable grant
      is recorded; assertion exchange and resource use are separate. Preview and approval require
      an eligible current human holder, one selected Casework profile, one selected source profile
      and current source visibility. The template fixes the task bounds; Casework derives proposal
      identity and subject values from the source. The officer reviews the fixed agent, client,
      resource, scopes, purpose, bounds, subjects and lifetime, plus requester tags and relying-party
      audience for Evidence tasks.
      The caller supplies only an exact template id and version. Missing task authority,
      stale item revision, changed binding, undisclosed facts, ineligible team or profile, and
      retired policy refuse approval. An exact idempotent retry, while the item revision, holding
      and source context remain unchanged, returns the original grant without extending its deadline.
      The template lifetime is capped at 900 seconds. Revocation is available to a currently eligible
      officer without requiring item ownership or another source read. Loss of holder eligibility,
      including release or reassignment; template retirement; source denial, concealment or moved
      binding; an ineligible Draft or terminal Rejected, Applied or Canceled source request; and a
      changed proposal or subject permanently invalidate an unexpired grant. Expiry makes the grant
      inactive. Source-item erasure makes it unavailable without recording invalidation. Temporary
      source failure refuses the current check without permanent invalidation.
    '''
    casework_staff -> casework_app 'Reviews task templates for a currently held source item'
    casework_app -> casework.api 'Requests eligible templates with both selected profiles'
    casework.api -> casework.access 'Authenticates the selected human profile and human-identity claim'
    casework.api -> casework.task_grants 'Checks current holding and directory eligibility, then requests fixed template bounds for source-derived proposal identity and subjects'
    casework.task_grants -> casework.breg_adapter 'Reads the exact proposal and governed subject fields'
    casework.breg_adapter -> breg.api 'Reads under the human bearer and selected BReg profile'
    breg.api -> casework.breg_adapter 'Returns current proposal identity and disclosed subject facts'
    casework.task_grants -> casework.api 'Returns only eligible immutable template previews'
    casework.api -> casework_app 'Presents fixed task bounds and any Evidence requester tags and relying-party audience'
    casework_staff -> casework_app 'Explicitly approves one template version'
    casework_app -> casework.api 'Sends template id and version with If-Match and idempotency key'
    casework.api -> casework.access 'Reauthenticates the selected human profile and human-identity claim'
    casework.api -> casework.task_grants 'Rechecks current holding, directory eligibility, revision and template'
    casework.task_grants -> casework.breg_adapter 'Rereads the current proposal and subject context'
    casework.breg_adapter -> breg.api 'Rereads under the human bearer and selected BReg profile'
    breg.api -> casework.breg_adapter 'Returns the current proposal identity and disclosed subject facts'
    casework.task_grants -> casework_postgres 'Atomically records a new grant or loads the original grant for an unchanged idempotent retry'
    casework.task_grants -> casework.api 'Returns the immutable grant without extending its deadline'
    casework.api -> casework_app 'Returns the grant id and original deadline'
  }

  dynamic view casework_source_event {
    title 'Casework / 04. A source event becomes current work'
    description '''
      A BReg request-lifecycle event is a signed invalidation and ordered revision hint.
      The adapter verifies its bounded CloudEvents envelope, signature, source, type and body,
      then discards source values and reasons. PostgreSQL deduplicates the hint and records
      synchronization intent. A worker later reads BReg with the configured service reader.
      The authoritative response, not the event, determines the occurrence, source binding,
      queue and clocks. Commit rechecks source generation and monotonic physical revision;
      at the same revision a changed strong representation ETag refreshes the occurrence.
      Periodic discovery and readback use the same path to repair missed events.
    '''
    breg.webhooks -> casework.api 'Sends a signed lifecycle hint after BReg commit'
    casework.api -> casework.coordination 'Constructs a bounded EventRequest and delegates intake'
    casework.coordination -> casework.breg_adapter 'Calls verify_transition through the injected SourceAdapter'
    casework.breg_adapter -> casework.coordination 'Returns a verified value-free subject, revision and dedupe hint'
    casework.coordination -> casework_postgres 'Records deduplicated synchronization intent'
    casework.workers -> casework_postgres 'Claims a bounded pending subject lease'
    casework.workers -> casework.breg_adapter 'Calls read_authoritative through the injected SourceAdapter'
    casework.breg_adapter -> breg.api 'Reads current work with the bounded service-reader profile'
    breg.api -> casework.breg_adapter 'Returns current request state, binding and configured projection'
    casework.workers -> casework_postgres 'Commits the observation, routing result and clock state under generation and revision fences'
  }

  dynamic view casework_source_action {
    title 'Casework / 05. A human action reaches its source'
    description '''
      The item must be held by this Staff actor at the expected Casework revision. Casework
      first rereads BReg under the human bearer and explicit source profile, so current source
      visibility and advertised operations control the action. The adapter compares the exact
      displayed source revision, proposal version, integrity and binding generation, then
      prepares an inert exact-action capsule without writing. Casework durably reserves the
      actor, both profiles, request hash, idempotency key, displayed binding and recovery
      evidence before the source POST. Recovery evidence contains no bearer or source content.
      A successful receipt leaves the item synchronizing until authoritative readback confirms
      current source state. Approval and application remain separate source operations. A reason
      unsupported by the selected approve or apply operation is refused before reservation. After
      reservation, only a validated problem from the actual initial POST can definitively close the
      attempt as a rejected request, missing record, unauthorized reviewer or no-longer-offered
      action. Transport failures, malformed problems and other inconclusive outcomes remain uncertain.
    '''
    casework_staff -> casework_app 'Confirms an advertised action for the claimed item'
    casework_app -> casework.api 'Sends both profiles, If-Match, displayed binding and idempotency key'
    casework.api -> casework.access 'Authenticates the selected human profile and human-identity claim'
    casework.api -> casework.coordination 'Checks current directory membership, claim ownership, item revision and actor-private draft'
    casework.coordination -> casework.breg_adapter 'Requests caller view and prepares the exact current action'
    casework.breg_adapter -> breg.api 'Rereads under the human bearer and selected BReg profile'
    breg.api -> casework.breg_adapter 'Returns current binding and source-advertised operations'
    casework.coordination -> casework_postgres 'Atomically reserves pending attempt, history, audit intent and synchronizing state'
    casework.coordination -> casework.breg_adapter 'Executes only the durably saved exact-action capsule'
    casework.breg_adapter -> breg.api 'Posts the promoted lifecycle action with its original source idempotency key'
    breg.api -> casework.breg_adapter 'Returns a validated source receipt'
    casework.coordination -> casework_postgres 'Commits completed attempt, receipt, history, audit intent and readback request'
  }

  dynamic view casework_attempt_recovery {
    title 'Casework / 06. Ambiguity and exact-attempt recovery'
    description '''
      Once source execution may have started, loss of a validated response is ambiguous.
      Casework records the attempt as uncertain and never invents a replacement action. Only
      the same actor and Casework profile can recover the exact capsule, using the same source
      profile and idempotency key under the same actor\'s current human bearer after current source
      visibility succeeds.
      A recovery response that is not a valid receipt remains uncertain, including a later
      refusal that cannot prove the original POST failed. Recovery leases fence concurrent
      callers. Even a validated refusal during recovery cannot prove that the initial POST did
      not commit. A valid receipt completes the attempt, queues authoritative readback and exits
      this sequence. The steps below follow the still-uncertain branch. If the source can no
      longer reveal the outcome, a Casework operator may preview and apply a settlement only
      after the execution lease expires and the source owner has confirmed applied or
      not-applied. That local settlement makes no BReg call and records its reason and decider
      in the same transaction as the state change.
    '''
    casework.breg_adapter -> casework.coordination 'Returns timeout, transport failure or invalid outcome after execution may have begun'
    casework.coordination -> casework_postgres 'Commits uncertain attempt, synchronizing item, history and audit intent'
    casework.api -> casework.coordination 'Same actor requests recovery by attempt or original key'
    casework.coordination -> casework_postgres 'Loads the actor-bound capsule and acquires an expired execution lease'
    casework.coordination -> casework.breg_adapter 'Requests recovery of the exact prepared action'
    casework.breg_adapter -> breg.api 'Replays through the maintained BReg client with the original idempotency key'
    breg.api -> casework.breg_adapter 'Does not return a conclusive valid receipt'
    casework.breg_adapter -> casework.coordination 'Reports the exact attempt outcome as still uncertain'
    casework.coordination -> casework_postgres 'Releases the recovery lease while retaining the uncertain attempt'
    casework_operator -> casework_cli 'After source-owner confirmation, previews one expired uncertain attempt settlement'
    casework_cli -> casework_postgres 'Checks exact attempt, lease and item state without writing'
    casework_operator -> casework_cli 'Applies the reviewed applied or not-applied settlement'
    casework_cli -> casework_postgres 'Atomically fences the old executor and records state, history and audit intent'
  }

  dynamic view casework_hosted_decision {
    title 'Casework / 07. A hosted decision returns a bounded result'
    description '''
      This path starts no BReg service. A Requester can create only a kind declared for its
      selected profile. The body supplies an opaque correlation reference and schema-validated
      display data; it cannot choose a person, team, outcomes, callback or external fetch.
      Acceptance pins the kind version, policy digest, display, queue and outcome vocabulary.
      A current human team member claims and decides under that pinned policy. The terminal
      event and protected raw actor accountability commit together, but the Requester feed
      receives only the declared outcome and stable opaque per-person actor reference. A current
      Supervisor for the queue can resolve one event through a separate audited route until accountability
      expiry. Requesters can add and page bounded notes while an item is active, or cancel their
      own item with a reason; cancellation produces a terminal event with no actor reference.
      Terminal payload and accountability retention are independent; idempotency
      tombstones preserve only request hashes and hashed bindings after payload expiry.
    '''
    casework_requester -> casework.api 'Creates a declared hosted kind with an idempotency key'
    casework.api -> casework.access 'Selects the Requester profile and issuer-qualified owner'
    casework.api -> casework.hosted 'Validates bounded display data against the declared kind'
    casework.hosted -> casework_postgres 'Commits item, pinned policy snapshot, history, audit intent and replay response'
    casework_staff -> casework_app 'Claims the visible hosted item and records a declared outcome'
    casework_app -> casework.api 'Sends revision-fenced claim and decision requests'
    casework.api -> casework.hosted 'Rechecks current team membership and pinned deciding profile'
    casework.hosted -> casework_postgres 'Atomically commits terminal event, stable opaque actor ref, protected accountability and audit intent'
    casework_requester -> casework.api 'Pages its own terminal feed'
    casework.api -> casework_requester 'Returns outcome and stable opaque per-person actor ref without raw human identity or reason'
    casework_supervisor -> casework_app 'Requests accountability for one retained terminal event'
    casework_app -> casework.api 'Calls the separate accountability route'
    casework.api -> casework.hosted 'Requests accountability for one terminal event'
    casework.hosted -> casework_postgres 'Checks Supervisor authority for a team serving the queue and records the audited read'
  }

  view casework_interoperability {
    title 'Casework / 08. Runtime interoperability'
    description '''
      The BReg adapter is optional and is the only implemented source protocol. Its service
      reader uses its own private_key_jwt client, exact reader profile, resource and scopes to
      discover, refresh and recheck bounded coordination facts; it cannot review or apply.
      Human caller reads and Casework-coordinated actions forward the ephemeral human bearer to
      BReg under an explicit source profile; Casework never stores that credential. Optional
      task grants create a parallel institutional-agent path outside the adapter: Casework signs
      a bounded assertion but does not issue the exchanged OAuth token or lend human authority.
      BReg remains the source of current record visibility, advertised operations and mutations.
      Signed webhook events accelerate synchronization but grant no visibility or action. Hosted
      decisions and the directory remain usable in a source-free deployment. Both runtimes keep
      separate data, packages, database roles, audit records and authorization decisions.
    '''
    include casework_app, casework_client, taskAgent, issuer, casework.api, casework.access, casework.coordination, casework.task_grants, casework.breg_adapter, casework_postgres, breg.api, breg.webhooks, breg.task_grants
  }

  view casework_authoring_operations {
    title 'Casework / 09. Authoring and operator artifacts'
    description '''
      caseworkctl checks one source-neutral project, including any optional immutable task
      templates, and packages only its policy plus exact imported source descriptions. For the
      implemented BReg adapter, source add invokes the same-version public bregctl only to check
      the registry project and explain change requests. caseworkctl previews exact additions,
      checks a staged copy, then atomically writes the authored BReg event, reader profile and
      development client files itself only when apply is requested. That authoring composition
      activates no package and provisions no authority. Production selection is an operator
      action: install the reviewed Casework package, supply runtime bindings and secrets, add
      task authority only when those templates are enabled, apply migrations with caseworkctl db
      migrate or the casework binary\'s migrate entry point, then start or roll out the runtime
      with its serve entry point. The caseworkctl dev command can run a retained local service over
      a BReg development session with token and task-exchange helpers. Doctor checks configuration,
      database, source, issuer and directory readiness, but source-reader readiness cannot attest that
      BReg webhook delivery is wired end to end. Retention erasure and uncertain-attempt
      settlement are separate preview-then-apply operator operations against Casework data.
    '''
    include casework_operator, casework_project, casework_cli, tooling, project, casework_package, casework_runtime_config, casework, casework_postgres
    global predicate casework_storage
  }
}
